@startuml NCO Frequency Calculation
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontSize 12

title Cálculo de Frecuencia NCO - De Velocidad a Pulsos

start

:Input: Velocity (mm/min)\nfrom M203 command;

:Constants:\n- STEPS_PER_MM = 320\n- FOSC = System clock frequency;

partition "Step 1: Convert velocity to step rate" {
  :Calculate steps_per_minute = velocity * STEPS_PER_MM;
  :Calculate steps_per_second = steps_per_minute / 60;
  
  note right
    Example: velocity = 1000 mm/min
    steps_per_min = 1000 * 320 = 320,000
    steps_per_sec = 320,000 / 60 = 5,333.33 Hz
    STEP frequency = 5.333 kHz
  end note
}

partition "Step 2: NCO Increment Calculation" {
  :NCO_increment = (steps_per_second * 2^NCO_resolution) / FOSC;
  
  note right
    NCO1INC = (F_step * 2^20) / FOSC
    
    For PIC16F18426:
    - NCO resolution = 20 bits
    - FOSC = 32 MHz (typical)
    
    Example:
    NCO1INC = (5333.33 * 1048576) / 32000000
    NCO1INC ≈ 175.6
    Round to: 176 (0x00B0)
  end note
}

partition "Step 3: Configure NCO1" {
  :Load NCO1INC register with calculated value;
  :Set NCO1 to pulse mode;
  :NCO1OUT routed to RC1 via PPS;
  :Enable NCO;
}

:Output: STEP pulse train at calculated frequency;

stop

---

[Function: nco_set_speed()]

skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor #E8F4F8
  BorderColor #0066CC
}

rectangle "
void nco_set_speed(uint16_t velocity_mm_min) {
    // Constants
    #define STEPS_PER_MM 320
    #define NCO_RESOLUTION 1048576  // 2^20
    
    // Calculate step frequency
    uint32_t steps_per_sec = (velocity_mm_min * STEPS_PER_MM) / 60;
    
    // Calculate NCO increment
    uint32_t nco_inc = (steps_per_sec * NCO_RESOLUTION) / FOSC;
    
    // Write to NCO1INC registers (24-bit)
    NCO1INCL = (nco_inc >> 16) & 0xFF;
    NCO1INCH = (nco_inc >> 8) & 0xFF;
    NCO1INC = nco_inc & 0xFF;
}
" as code_example

note right of code_example
  **Current Issues:**
  
  1. Fixed frequency (no acceleration)
  2. Some frequencies cause motor resonance
  3. No ramp-up/ramp-down
  4. May lose steps at certain speeds
  
  **TODO:**
  - Add variable NCO increment in ISR for ramps
  - Test and map problematic frequencies
  - Add filtering or skip ranges
end note

